# Line endings, and one claim that depends on them
# ================================================
#
# `/api/federated/<STATE>` promises to return a state node "byte for byte", and
# `_national.json` publishes the SHA-256 and byte count of every one of those
# files so a reviewer can check that promise against the repository. That is the
# centrepiece of the federated argument: it asks to be verified rather than
# believed.
#
# Git's default on Windows quietly breaks it. With `core.autocrlf=true` — which
# is the default the Git for Windows installer sets — every LF in a text file
# becomes CRLF on checkout, so a fresh clone's `src/data/federated/10.json` is
# 32,615 bytes where the digest describes 30,223. Nothing about the data
# changed; the bytes did. `npm test` went red on a clean clone of a green
# commit, which is the worst possible way for a reviewer to meet this project.
#
# So the payloads whose BYTES are claimed are marked `-text`: git stores and
# restores them verbatim on every platform, and the digest means the same thing
# to everyone.
#
# Everything else is left alone deliberately. Source files have no byte-level
# claim over them, developers on Windows are used to CRLF working copies, and
# renormalising the whole tree would produce an enormous diff to solve a problem
# that does not exist.

# --- the federated nodes and the prior that hashes them ----------------------
src/data/federated/*.json    -text

# --- the other committed artefacts a figure is read out of -------------------
# Not digest-bearing, but they are compared byte-for-byte between runs by
# `scripts/test-determinism.mts` and by the snapshot build's own idempotence
# check, and a checkout that changes their length makes those comparisons
# describe the platform rather than the pipeline.
src/data/*.json              -text
src/data/districts/*.json    -text
docs/*.json                  -text

# The Hindi audio fixture the voice rehearsal and the demo recording play.
# Binary, and it has been corrupted by an EOL conversion before.
scripts/fixtures/*.mp3        binary

# The IDSP bulletins kept as parser fixtures. test-idsp checks each one's SHA-256
# against the manifest, so a conversion would fail the suite on a fresh clone.
scripts/fixtures/idsp/*.pdf   binary
data/idsp/*.json              -text
